<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chap-integration" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Integration</title>
<introduction>

<blockquote>
<p><em><q>I take the bills and coins out of my pocket and give them to the creditor in the order I find them until I have reached the total sum. This is the Riemann integral. But I can proceed differently. After I have taken all the money out of my pocket I order the bills and coins according to identical values and then I pay the several heaps one after the other to the creditor. This is my integral.</q></em></p>
<attribution>
Henri Lebesgue
</attribution>
</blockquote>

<p>In this chapter we will define integrals of measurable functions and prove main key theorems on integration. We will play particular attention to integration of functions on the real line (with respect to Lebesgue measure) and integration of functions on closed intervals <m>[a,b] \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m> with respect to Borel measures.</p>
<p>To build up the theory of integration in <xref ref="sec-int-non-neg"/> and <xref ref="sec-integration"/>. We will define integration first for simple functions; then non-negative measurable functions; and finally for more general measurable functions.
We will prove main theorems of integration, including the <xref ref="thm-MCT" text="custom">Monotone Convergence Theorem</xref> and the <xref ref="thm-LDC" text="custom">Lebesgue Dominated Convergence Theorem</xref>. 
These two theorems directly deal with the question of interchanging limits and integrals, as discussed in <xref ref="chap-intro"/>.
</p>

<p>
    In <xref ref="sec-int-Riemann"/> we compare the Lebesgue integral and the Riemann integral, for integrals on the real line. For functions on closed bounded intervals in the real line, we show that all Riemann integrable functions are Lebesgue integrable, and that the two integrals agree. Thus, on closed bounded intervals, the Lebesgue integral is a more robust form of integration than the Riemann integral, as asked for in <xref ref="chap-intro"/>.
    However, in <xref ref="ex-not-lebesgue-int-1"/> and <xref ref="ex-not-lebesgue-int-2"/> we show that there are Riemann integrable functions on the whole of the real line, which are not Lebesgue integrable.
</p>    
</introduction>


